WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.339
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.540
TIMOTHY SEHN: Hi, Diane.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.331
DIANE GREENE: You know,
we'll all sit down.

00:00:11.331 --> 00:00:14.730
But I don't know if all of you
need to stand up and sit down

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:15.280
for a second.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.728
It's kind of cold in here.

00:00:16.728 --> 00:00:20.170
And give you all a-- you know,
what is it, the seventh inning

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:22.380
break or something?

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.360
TIMOTHY SEHN: This is not LA.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.970
CHRIS O'NEILL: Is this
because we're Canadian, Diane?

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.110
DIANE GREENE: OK.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.020
All right, we can sit.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.470
So yeah, I'm really-- you
know, what fun to have

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:40.270
Chris, CEO of Evernote, and
Tim, head of-- VP of engineering

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.690
at Snap.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:46.800
And you guys do have
a bit in common.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.947
Snap has 100 million
active users, at least,

00:00:49.947 --> 00:00:51.030
according to your website.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.363
TIMOTHY SEHN: I think the
last published number was 150,

00:00:53.363 --> 00:00:54.570
but we'll see.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.000
DIANE GREENE: We
think maybe more.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.070
And then you have your very
popular application with--

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:03.340
or service with-- 200 million.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:10.550
And I guess you are both, just
like our previous speaker,

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:11.669
Canucks.

00:01:11.669 --> 00:01:12.460
CHRIS O'NEILL: Yes.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:12.950
TIMOTHY SEHN: Yes.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:13.658
DIANE GREENE: Yes

00:01:13.658 --> 00:01:14.810
CHRIS O'NEILL: We are.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.040
Diane, we'll try to
keep the gloves on,

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.242
and we'll keep the hockey
references to a minimum.

00:01:20.242 --> 00:01:20.950
DIANE GREENE: OK.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.090
CHRIS O'NEILL: But you have
to forgive us if we disappear.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.048
So Canada is playing in
the World Cup of Hockey

00:01:25.048 --> 00:01:26.010
later tonight.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:26.510
And--

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:27.170
DIANE GREENE: Oh, OK.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.040
CHRIS O'NEILL: Yeah,
right, against Europe.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.320
And if we don't watch it, I
think the Canadian government

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.610
might revoke our passports.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:35.590
DIANE GREENE: OK.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:38.220
So you two have taken
different paths to the cloud.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.030
Evernote was on-prem.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.060
You're just starting
to move to the cloud.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.110
CHRIS O'NEILL: Yeah.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:44.901
DIANE GREENE: When you
arrived at Snapchat,

00:01:44.901 --> 00:01:47.099
it was running on
Google App Engine.

00:01:47.099 --> 00:01:48.140
TIMOTHY SEHN: Absolutely.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:52.410
DIANE GREENE: And you've
built systems both on premise

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.120
and in the cloud.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.830
What have you
found about working

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.570
in the cloud that's different?

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:58.310
Is it better?

00:01:58.310 --> 00:01:59.380
Is it worse?

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:00.684
TIMOTHY SEHN: Sure.

00:02:00.684 --> 00:02:02.100
To answer the last
question first,

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.820
I think it's
unquestionably better.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:10.800
I like to tell a story about
when I arrived at Snapchat.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.980
So I'm employee 18.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.370
When I started there, my
engineering team was 10.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.820
The most experienced
engineer on the team

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:24.160
had three years of
engineering experience.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.872
The application was doing--

00:02:25.872 --> 00:02:27.080
DIANE GREENE: Fresh thinking.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.038
TIMOTHY SEHN: Yeah, yeah,
fresh thinking, yeah.

00:02:29.038 --> 00:02:33.240
So the application was doing
between 200,000 and 300,000

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.150
queries per second
to the back end.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.530
Not a single one of those
people carried a pager.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:43.810
So they just assumed
it would work.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:49.590
And we were having 5%
week over week growth.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:52.040
And Google was chugging
along in the background.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.921
Now, I'm not-- I'm not going to
pretend there weren't problems.

00:02:55.921 --> 00:02:58.420
Once I got there, I spent a
year working with Google to kind

00:02:58.420 --> 00:02:59.950
of optimize and get better.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:04.470
But I was certainly
not-- the application

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:07.100
wasn't down all the time.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.270
You think back to the early
days of Twitter's hyper-growth,

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.340
and everyone would
joke about fail whales.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:14.510
We had none of those.

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:19.509
And so I apologize to anyone
from Twitter in the audience

00:03:19.509 --> 00:03:20.300
If there is anyone.

00:03:22.939 --> 00:03:23.730
DIANE GREENE: Yeah.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.500
You told me, I think,
when I met you in January,

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.500
that you had 16 sysops people.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.384
TIMOTHY SEHN: So
I had 10 people,

00:03:36.384 --> 00:03:37.800
and I operated the
servers myself.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.610
DIANE GREENE: Oh, you were
the only sysops person.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.193
TIMOTHY SEHN: Yeah, I would page
myself whenever it went down.

00:03:42.193 --> 00:03:44.000
And then I would cut
a ticket to Google,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.840
and we'd work
together to fix it.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.270
My friend and colleague from
Amazon, Aaron, joined me.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:53.560
And then we would do it
together, the two of us,

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:55.790
until about April or May.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:58.040
So for about the first eight
months I was at Snapchat,

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:03.600
I was the lone operator
and VP of engineering.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.570
DIANE GREENE: Yeah,
still laughing.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:09.740
Chris, you actually
came from Google.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.010
I know you value our technology.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:15.020
You value our innovation.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.529
What is the technical
innovation that you're

00:04:17.529 --> 00:04:19.160
looking for from Google?

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.640
CHRIS O'NEILL: Yeah, so
I've been at Evernote just

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:22.360
over a year.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.110
And in the first
observation was,

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.170
we were trying to
do too many things.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.640
And in this world, I don't
care what scale you're at,

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.060
you have to do a few things
and do them world class.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.510
Like, we operate in every
country in the world.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.629
So we really just did a
hard assessment of, like,

00:04:35.629 --> 00:04:37.670
what things are we going
to really double down on

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:38.794
and what things are we not.

00:04:38.794 --> 00:04:41.820
And operating a physical
data center just

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.410
was clearly not something we
wanted to differentiate around.

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.570
So once we'd made
that decision, we

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:48.690
asked ourselves a couple
questions, maybe three.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:51.590
We said, OK, what's the
next three to five years

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.060
hold in terms of a
computing platform?

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.630
Who's the best in
the world at that?

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:58.105
And then, three, and
perhaps most importantly,

00:04:58.105 --> 00:04:59.480
what are the
capabilities that we

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:01.060
need to really,
really meaningfully

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.084
differentiate and really
deliver value for our users?

00:05:04.084 --> 00:05:06.000
The first two were
relatively straightforward.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.333
AI and machine learning are
the next computing platform,

00:05:08.333 --> 00:05:10.185
there's no question
in our minds.

00:05:10.185 --> 00:05:11.560
And I'm a little
biased, perhaps,

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:12.976
from spending some
time at Google.

00:05:12.976 --> 00:05:14.957
But Google clearly is
the leader in terms of AI

00:05:14.957 --> 00:05:16.720
and machine learning at scale.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.360
The third question was
about, what capabilities

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.109
do we need to marry
with the user's data

00:05:22.109 --> 00:05:23.400
that they trust with us, right?

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.440
We have 5 billion notes and
5 billion more attachments.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:29.480
This is people's very private
narrative of their life.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.160
So how do we combine
that information with ML,

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.080
essentially, to unlock
some real user experiences?

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.620
And we like to say our
mission is to unlock

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.160
the potential of ideas.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.950
So really, that's about
removing all friction.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:46.735
So step one, for us, was voice,
a very practical implication.

00:05:46.735 --> 00:05:49.110
I think we've hit a tipping
point when it comes to voice.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:53.990
And Google has disclosed, 20%
of searches come through voice.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:56.232
So we really want
to capture ideas

00:05:56.232 --> 00:05:58.190
at the speed at which
people come up with them.

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:00.410
So voice is one big bet for us.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:03.091
And we're really excited
about the NLP progress

00:06:03.091 --> 00:06:04.090
that you guys have made.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.650
So we think that's one
logical intersection.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.870
And then, once people put
information of these ideas

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.690
into Evernote, it's
a very manual process

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.270
for organizing today.

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:16.230
And we think that the power of
what we saw just a little bit

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:18.810
about that powers Google
Photos, for example,

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.140
would really help
users, as I say,

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:22.720
organize without
really organizing.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.650
It's like getting a six-pack
without going to the gym.

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:29.260
We can combine that
technology to help

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:30.870
people organize their content.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.370
And then last but not least
is search or retrieval.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.050
We really want to help people
surface the information

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:38.290
that they put in Evernote
at just the right time.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:40.410
So when we thought
about, who's the leader,

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:42.450
and who could help
us with capabilities,

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:45.155
it was kind of a
no-brainer in the end.

00:06:45.155 --> 00:06:48.270
And you, frankly,
were an intangible,

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.400
because you're committed to win.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:51.580
And it was clear.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:55.200
When we actually did look at
the intangible decision factors,

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.450
it was not even close in terms
of your ability and your desire

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:01.090
to win in this game,
even though you're

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:03.800
coming from a slight
disadvantage in terms

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:05.050
of when you started.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:06.791
DIANE GREENE: Yeah,
you're both talking

00:07:06.791 --> 00:07:08.540
about something kind
of interesting, which

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:15.290
is that for a longest time,
when you built a company,

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.645
you had to build absolutely
everything yourself.

00:07:17.645 --> 00:07:19.270
CHRIS O'NEILL: We
didn't have a choice.

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:20.645
10 years ago,
there was no cloud.

00:07:20.645 --> 00:07:21.357
There was no AWS.

00:07:21.357 --> 00:07:22.190
DIANE GREENE: Right.

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:23.570
So now, all of a
sudden, you're going,

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:26.160
wait a minute, we don't have
to build everything ourselves.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.530
And then, look at all the things
you want to take advantage of.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:36.150
And these Stanford undergrads,
didn't even occur to them

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:37.674
to build everything themselves.

00:07:37.674 --> 00:07:39.340
TIMOTHY SEHN: They
took the passive path

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:40.300
of least resistance.

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:40.870
DIANE GREENE: Yeah.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:43.161
TIMOTHY SEHN: Frankly, for
me, it's about focus, right?

00:07:43.161 --> 00:07:44.953
And I think that's what
he's talking about.

00:07:44.953 --> 00:07:47.036
When you don't have to
worry about infrastructure,

00:07:47.036 --> 00:07:48.490
and you don't have
to worry about,

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:53.500
is that rack going to land on
the front doors of your data

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:58.590
center so your network
engineer can cable it up,

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:00.410
and you can focus on
innovating on product,

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:05.710
you end up with just spending
more of your own cycles

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:10.230
and your team's cycles on
innovation, which is, I think,

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:12.505
where you end up
winning in the end.

00:08:12.505 --> 00:08:14.880
CHRIS O'NEILL: They're winning
because of speed, I think.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.752
And it's not just because
of selfies and photos.

00:08:18.752 --> 00:08:20.460
It's actually because
you guys are really

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:21.751
innovating with product, right?

00:08:21.751 --> 00:08:24.310
If you really ask
yourself who's pushing out

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:27.047
product at the highest quality
in that game, it's Snap.

00:08:27.047 --> 00:08:28.630
TIMOTHY SEHN: And
we're proud of that.

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:30.338
And we owe a lot of
that to Google Cloud,

00:08:30.338 --> 00:08:32.600
honestly, and have for
the last three years.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.629
We would not be
able to do what we

00:08:34.629 --> 00:08:36.559
have been able to do
without Google Cloud.

00:08:36.559 --> 00:08:38.559
DIANE GREENE: And our
site reliability engineers

00:08:38.559 --> 00:08:39.230
love you guys.

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:41.438
TIMOTHY SEHN: It's a love-hate
relationship, I think.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:46.970
DIANE GREENE: They
pay attention, right?

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:50.280
TIMOTHY SEHN: Yeah, they
absolutely pay attention.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:53.170
DIANE GREENE: So if you were to
look back, Tim, with all that

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:55.630
experience, and you
were your younger self,

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:58.830
is there any advice, if you
were just moving to the cloud

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:02.000
or doing things differently?

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.430
TIMOTHY SEHN: One of the
things I've really learned

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:09.880
is, managed services
make a big difference.

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:14.530
And depending on how
much state is preserved

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:17.470
dictates my desire for how
managed I want them to be.

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:22.320
So for a service where I
want large amounts of data

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.610
to live forever, I prefer that
to be as managed as I can.

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:28.010
And as I move up through
the caching layer

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:31.050
and into the serving layer,
I'm willing to tolerate

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:33.390
less and less managed--
or less or less

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:38.480
management-- of the services,
because it becomes easier,

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:41.680
as an engineer, to do that.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:48.550
And so I would reinforce that to
my younger self and to others.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:56.550
I think it's tempting,
as an engineer,

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:58.360
to want to control
your own destiny,

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:00.430
and take matters
into your own hands,

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:04.290
and know exactly how your
replication is working.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.110
And it takes a
little bit of courage

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:09.840
to let that go, and give
that to someone else,

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.480
and just trust that
the data storage

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:16.510
provider or the front
end service provider

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:19.900
will be able to take
care of that for you

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:23.810
and take care of it better than
you possibly do it yourself.

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:26.310
And so just lean into that.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.000
Again, this option wasn't
available 10 years ago.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:30.250
You had to do it all yourself.

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:32.180
And so it exists now.

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:33.920
And the companies
that are going to be

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:36.656
able to move fast, and
execute, and win, I think,

00:10:36.656 --> 00:10:38.280
are going to be the
companies that take

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:39.804
advantage of that advantage.

00:10:39.804 --> 00:10:41.470
DIANE GREENE: And who
would have thought

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:45.790
that a camera company like Snap
would need this infrastructure?

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:48.880
I was hoping you would
wear some spectacles today.

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:50.810
TIMOTHY SEHN: It's
not unveiled yet.

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:53.490
So stay tuned next few weeks.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.240
CHRIS O'NEILL: I think
if you frisked him,

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:57.567
you could maybe
find a pair, Diane.

00:10:57.567 --> 00:10:59.150
DIANE GREENE: And
what about Evernote?

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:02.230
What's next for Evernote
over the next few years?

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:04.220
CHRIS O'NEILL: So it's
been a great year.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:07.480
We've really invested in a bunch
of different areas, primarily

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:08.440
the team.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:10.480
So I fundamentally
believe the team

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:12.956
you build is the company you
build, and the best team wins.

00:11:12.956 --> 00:11:14.830
DIANE GREENE: Yeah,
you've made huge progress

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:15.770
in the last year, yeah.

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:17.436
CHRIS O'NEILL: Yeah,
so it's been great.

00:11:17.436 --> 00:11:19.020
And we've also
invested significantly

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:20.394
in the technical
platform, right?

00:11:20.394 --> 00:11:22.140
And GCP's a big part
of that, of course.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:24.764
But there are many other things
under the hood that we've done.

00:11:24.764 --> 00:11:27.030
And we're just so thrilled
to start unleashing,

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:30.030
on a regular cadence, stuff
that people will start to see.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:32.217
I use the metaphor of
an iceberg sometimes.

00:11:32.217 --> 00:11:34.550
A lot of what we've been doing
has been below the water.

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:38.020
So whether it's our search
technology, our editing,

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.277
or our sync technology,
these are all new things.

00:11:40.277 --> 00:11:42.860
So people are going to start to
see those things in the coming

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:43.360
weeks.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:47.290
And our users are really
excited in the early tests.

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:50.960
I think, more strategically,
if we look out a couple more

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:53.750
years, we've been a great
single player product.

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:56.470
And we really find-- and
there's appetite for us

00:11:56.470 --> 00:11:58.264
to help impact teams.

00:11:58.264 --> 00:12:00.430
So nothing great in the
world happens without teams.

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:02.388
And you saw a great
announcement from you guys.

00:12:02.388 --> 00:12:04.260
We believe in that future too.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:05.795
So how do we make
that migration--

00:12:05.795 --> 00:12:07.860
it Is a fifth of
our business today--

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:10.040
without actually investing
very heavily in that?

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:12.229
So you can expect us to do that.

00:12:12.229 --> 00:12:14.770
And I think that's another part
of the decision factor for us

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:18.294
too, was, when you try to
go win in the workplace,

00:12:18.294 --> 00:12:19.210
the stakes are higher.

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:20.860
The stakes are higher
in terms of security,

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:22.985
in terms of compliance,
and all these other things.

00:12:22.985 --> 00:12:26.762
And a lot of those things will
come as an added bonus for us.

00:12:26.762 --> 00:12:29.220
So we don't have to, again,
think about all those features.

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:30.921
You guys can help us with that.

00:12:30.921 --> 00:12:32.670
But we really want to
win in the workplace

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.004
and really help make teams 30%--

00:12:34.004 --> 00:12:35.753
DIANE GREENE: We totally
want to help you.

00:12:35.753 --> 00:12:37.280
We want to work
with you with apps.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:39.937
I do think Tim cares about
privacy and security.

00:12:39.937 --> 00:12:41.340
TIMOTHY SEHN: Absolutely.

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:43.210
DIANE GREENE: But
yes, absolutely, we'll

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:46.750
all-- the stakes,
the access controls

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:49.610
get more complicated in the
enterprise, that's for sure.

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:50.580
CHRIS O'NEILL: They do.

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:51.663
DIANE GREENE: Thanks, Tim.

00:12:51.663 --> 00:12:52.610
Thanks, Chris.

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:53.650
Thank you very much.

00:12:53.650 --> 00:12:54.210
CHRIS O'NEILL: Thank you, Diane.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:54.510
TIMOTHY SEHN: Thank you, Diane.

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:54.790
Appreciate it.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:55.220
Bye.

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:55.928
DIANE GREENE: OK.

00:12:55.928 --> 00:12:57.600
[APPLAUSE]

